 ## How it works
-**Inputs:** Ray origin, ray direction, voxel grid data organized in a Brick Tree, and a maximum traversal depth.
-**Outputs:** Intersection point, surface normal, and color.
+**Inputs:** Ray origin, ray direction, voxel grid data organized in a Brick Tree, and a maximum traversal depth [G].
+**Outputs:** Intersection point, surface normal, and color [G].
 
 **Data Structures:**
-*   **Node:** Contains a 64-bit integer bitmask. Each bit $i$ (where $0 \le i < 64$) corresponds to one of the 64 child nodes. If bit $i$ is set (1), the child node contains non-empty voxels and is subdivided. If bit $i$ is unset (0), the child node is empty and can be skipped [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Tree Hierarchy:** The root node covers the entire voxel volume. Each node is subdivided into a 4×4×4 grid of children. This structure builds on [Voxel Bricks](5794-voxel-bricks.md), which groups voxels into 8×8×8 bricks; the Brick Tree operates on these bricks or similar macro-cells [S1, unofficial].
